<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Asmussen Super Prestige: a Danish custom time-trial bicycle from around 1988 with funny-bike geometry and Campagnolo components."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/asmussen-super-prestige/hero.webp"/><title>Asmussen Super Prestige · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/asmussen-super-prestige/"/><main class="atala-page"><header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">In the collection · Aarhus, Denmark · around 1988</p><h1>Asmussen<br/><em>Super Prestige</em></h1><p>A Danish time-trial bicycle from the years when a small front wheel, low cockpit and crouched rider promised a path through the air.</p></header><figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Yellow and black Asmussen time-trial bicycle"/><figcaption>The unequal wheels create its funny-bike profile. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure><section class="atala-facts"><div><strong>c. 1988</strong><span>Estimated period</span></div><div><strong>Aarhus</strong><span>Danish custom build</span></div><div><strong>Funny bike</strong><span>Time-trial geometry</span></div><div><strong>Campagnolo</strong><span>Croce d’Aune / C-Record</span></div></section><section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Gunnar Asmussen</p><h2>From Olympic gold to his own bicycle shop</h2></div><div><p>Asmussen won Olympic team-pursuit gold in 1968 with Per Lyngemark, Reno Olsen and Mogens Frey. He later ran Gunnar Asmussens Cykelsportscenter in Aarhus for more than three decades.</p><p>Who cut and brazed this particular frame is not documented. It clearly shows, however, how international aerodynamic experiments became a Danish custom build bearing Asmussen’s name.</p></div></section><section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">Funny bike</p><h2>Rider first, bicycle second</h2></div><div><p>A smaller front wheel lowered the bars while a full-size rear wheel retained familiar gearing. The sloping top tube and unequal wheels looked odd beside a traditional racer, but their purpose was serious: reducing the rider’s frontal area.</p><p>The polished Campagnolo parts, toe clips and frame-mounted shifters reveal a transition in which aerodynamics advanced faster than ergonomics.</p></div></section><section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><img src={`${images}/brancale-helmet.webp`} alt="White Brancale aerodynamic helmet"/><figcaption>The period helmet is displayed with the bicycle, though not documented as its original partner.</figcaption></figure><div><h2>From wheel to head</h2><p>Its long tail continued the line from head to back. Rider, helmet, bars and wheels were increasingly treated as a single shape.</p></div></section><section class="koga-specs"><div><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Custom time-trial bicycle</dd></div><div><dt>Geometry</dt><dd>Small front wheel and low cockpit</dd></div><div><dt>Components</dt><dd>Campagnolo Croce d’Aune / C-Record era</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>In the collection</dd></div></dl></section><section class="atala-closing"><p>A short-lived piece of Danish craft from before regulations made racing bicycles more uniform.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1980-1989/`}>View the period →</a></div></section></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
